\begin_layout Section
Familiarity Measurement with the Tree Edit Distance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The explainability distance to the potential candidates are resulted in
 the following considerations for a complexity measurement: 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "xaiMeasurementRequirements"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Prerequisites for a distance measure 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $d(x,y)\ge0$
\end_inset

 (non-negativity)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $d(x,y)=0\Leftrightarrow x=y$
\end_inset

 (identity of indiscernibles) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $d(x,y)=d(y,x)$
\end_inset

 (symmetry) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $d(x,y)\le d(x,z)+d(z,y)$
\end_inset

 (triangle inequality) 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Measure small differences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Univariate or low-dimansional multivariate formula, based on a few paradigms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Not fundamentally limiting the Evolution process
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tree edit distance (TED) meets all these requirements and is also a
 well-known problem from theoretical computer science.
 It measures the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

distance from two labeled ordered trees T and T' by the minimum cost sequence
 of edit operations needed to transform T into T'
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "tedOldestPaper"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 Edits are the elemental changes 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\text{delete},\text{insert,\text{modify}\}}$
\end_inset

 that can be applied to a tree.
 
\end_layout

